        "id": 8704
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-07-09T15:47:01Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Is this heap allocation and data copy (associated with `to_vec`) necessary?  IIUC `\u0026[u8]` provides `Read + Seek`, so we should be able to use it directly.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 284,
        "startChar": 67,
        "endLine": 284,
        "endChar": 75
      },
      "revId": "d4d3d48d3873bd086a207a811f221186d80e61da",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "e299730b_0f6ffe66",
        "filename": "experimental/rust_bmp/ffi/FFI.rs",
        "patchSetId": 6
      },
      "lineNbr": 284,
      "author": {
        "id": 4159917
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-07-09T16:45:08Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "A borrowed `Cursor\u003c\u0026[u8]\u003e` would work only if the borrow were limited to this function. Here the `Reader` is returned to C++ and retained by the codec after the FFI call returns, so Rust needs to own the bytes unless we introduce an unsafe lifetime contract tied to the C++ `SkData`/`SkStream`.\n\nFor this CL I kept the safe Rust-owned copy and documented that `data.to_vec()` is intentional because `Reader` outlives the FFI call.",
      "parentUuid": "0223106c_804bb84a",
